    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Reconnaissance / Wireless SSID Enumeration", "technique": "T1465", "what_happened": "At 2026-03-24 01:08:11 UTC, auditd on UBUNTU-CONFROOM-02 (10.40.18.52) logged execve events for wireless scanning utilities — nmcli device wifi list and iwlist scan — enumerating visible SSIDs in range. This is the recon step that precedes an evil-twin: the operator must observe the legitimate corporate SSID (and its encryption profile) before cloning it. Wazuh rule.groups carried 'audit' + 'wireless_recon' and rule.description referenced wifi scan tooling.", "chain_reason": "auditd execve rule fired for command-line tools nmcli/iwlist on agent.name=UBUNTU-CONFROOM-02 within the incident window — the SSID-enumeration precursor to rogue-AP setup.", "first_seen": "2026-03-24T01:08:11+0000"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Resource Development / Rogue AP Configuration Staging", "technique": "T1465", "what_happened": "At 01:11:02 UTC, Wazuh FIM (syscheck) detected creation of /tmp/evil_twin_20260324_011102/hostapd.conf on UBUNTU-CONFROOM-02. Content analysis flagged the file: it declares ssid=CorpWiFi (an exact clone of the corporate SSID), bssid=00:11:22:33:44:55 (a spoofed MAC distinct from any registered corporate AP), and crucially auth_algs=1 with NO wpa= / wpa_key_mgmt= directives — i.e. OPEN authentication replacing the corporate WPA2-Enterprise profile. A second file /tmp/evil_twin_20260324_011102/dnsmasq.conf was created in the same directory declaring a rogue DHCP range 192.168.200.0/24 and address=/#/192.168.200.1 (wildcard DNS redirect — captive-portal / credential-harvest infrastructure). Wazuh rule 92950 'Rogue AP config file created' [T1465] fired at critical.", "chain_reason": "syscheck event=added for path matching /tmp/evil_twin_*/hostapd.conf on agent.name=UBUNTU-CONFROOM-02, content carrying the corporate SSID string 'CorpWiFi' + open-auth profile, immediately followed by dnsmasq.conf in the same staging directory — the rogue-AP-plus-DNS-redirect infrastructure fingerprint.", "first_seen": "2026-03-24T01:11:02+0000"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Initial Access / Adversary-in-the-Middle — Evil Twin Activation", "technique": "T1557", "what_happened": "At 01:11:29 UTC, auditd logged an execve event for /usr/sbin/hostapd on UBUNTU-CONFROOM-02 with the staging directory as working directory. Wazuh rule 92951 'hostapd invocation detected' [T1465] fired at high. This is the activation of the rogue access point — the host is now broadcasting a duplicate 'CorpWiFi' SSID on the open-auth profile, positioned to capture any corporate client that auto-associates to the stronger signal. The combination FIM(open-auth clone config) + process(hostapd) on the same host inside a 30-second window is the unambiguous evil-twin activation signature.", "chain_reason": "auditd execve for image=/usr/sbin/hostapd on agent.name=UBUNTU-CONFROOM-02 at 01:11:29, ~27s after the rule 92950 FIM event on the cloned hostapd.conf — the config-stage→activate sequence that defines a live evil twin.", "first_seen": "2026-03-24T01:11:29+0000"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Credential Access / Post-Association Harvest Indication", "technique": "T1557", "what_happened": "Between 01:11:29 and 01:13:55 UTC, Wazuh logged follow-on alerts referencing the rogue BSSID and SSID: a wireless authentication event noting association to an unknown/unregistered BSSID for the CorpWiFi SSID, and rule.description text referencing the captive-portal redirect domain. The timing — client association to the open-auth twin within ~3 minutes of activation, followed by credential-submission indicators — matches the playbook's evil-twin→credential-harvest progression. No legitimate change-management ticket exists for any AP deployment in the Level-2 conference room.", "chain_reason": "Wazuh alerts in the 01:11-01:13 window carrying the rogue bssid string 00:11:22:33:44:55 and SSID CorpWiFi in rule.description, on the same incident timeline as the rule 92950/92951 chain — the credential-harvest follow-on of the evil-twin.", "first_seen": "2026-03-24T01:13:55+0000"}
